\h Leviticus
\toc1 Leviticus
\toc2 Leviticus
\toc3 Lev.
\mt2 The handbook of
\mt1 Sacred Living
\mt3 commonly known as
\mt1 Leviticus
\is1 Introduction
\ip The Latin word \bk Leviticus\bk* means ‘concerning the Levites’, who were the tribe of workers and priests who God chose to serve him at the altar and in other religious ceremonies. Hebrew readers call this document ‘Vayikrā’ which is the first word in the Hebrew and means ‘And he called’ (Yahweh called Mosheh/Moses). Some European and other translations call it ‘The third book of Mosheh/Moses’.
\ip It's presumed that Mosheh wrote this document down while they were all camped there at Mt. Sinai as mentioned in a few places: 1:1, 4:1, 6:1,24, and of 8:1.
\ip This document focuses on the proper values and behaviour that the Israelis should observe as Yahweh's chosen and sacred people. That involved staying ‘clean’ as per Yahweh's values by carefully following his detailed instructions, and by avoiding wickedness.
\ip Some of the instructions apply particularly to the priests (Aharon's/Aaron's descendants) and to the remainder of their own tribe (the Levites that this document is often named after) who served the priests in their sacred ceremonies.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Instructions about offerings and sacrifices \ior 1:1–7:38\ior*
\io1 The dedication of Aharon and his sons as Yahweh's priests \ior 8:1–10:20\ior*
\io1 Instructions about ‘clean’ and ‘unclean’ \ior 11:1–15:33\ior*
\io1 The day for getting right with Yahweh \ior 16:1-34\ior*
\io1 Instructions for living and worshipping as a sacred people \ior 17:1–27:34\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Procedures for burnt sacrifices
\rem /s1 Sacrifices Burned Whole; The Burnt Offering; Offerings and sacrifices; Procedures for the Burnt Offering
\p
\v 1 Yahweh¦46173 \add ≈summoned\add* Mosheh¦46171 \add !(Moses¦46171)\add* to the \add ≈sacred¦46175\add* tent¦46175 and¦46172 \add ≈told him\add*,
\v 2 “Tell¦46183 the¦46182 \add ≈Israelis¦46182\add* that when anyone¦46186 presents¦46189 an offering¦46205 to Yahweh¦46192, it must¦46184 be presented¦46191 from their \add ?own\add* herd¦46198 or flock¦46201.
\v 3 If it's from¦46211 the herd¦46213, it must be a bull with no¦46215 defects¦46215. It must be presented¦46223 \add ≈in front¦46226 of\add* the entrance¦46219 to the \add ≈sacred¦46221\add* tent¦46221 to be acceptable¦46225 to Yahweh¦46227.
\v 4 \add @The owner\add* must¦46229 place his¦46230 hand¦46230 on¦46231 the¦46232 head¦46232 of¦46232 that offering¦46233 to be burnt¦46233, and¦46229 then¦46229 it¦46234 will¦46234 be accepted¦46234 \add ≈make¦46236 him right with Yahweh\add*.
\v 5 Then¦46239 he¦46239 must slaughter¦46239 the \add ≈bull in¦46244 front¦46244\add* of Yahweh¦46245, and the priests¦46249 (Aharon's¦46248 \add !(Aaron's¦46248)\add* descendants), must present¦46246 the blood¦46252 and splash it all around¦46260 the altar¦46259 which¦46261 is¦46263 by the entrance¦46263 of the \add ≈sacred¦46264\add* tent¦46264.
\v 6 Then¦46267 \add @the presenter\add* must¦46267 skin¦46267 the \add ≈bull\add* and¦46271 cut¦46271 it¦46272 into¦46273 pieces¦46273,
\v 7 \add ≈Meanwhile,\add* the priest¦46278 Aharon's¦46277 sons¦46276 must¦46275 put¦46275 fire on the altar¦46282 and¦46275 arrange¦46283 wood on the fire,
\v 8 and then¦46289 \add *they¦46289\add* must¦46289 arrange¦46289 the pieces¦46294 (including¦46298 the head¦46297 and the fat portions) on top of the burning¦46307 wood on the altar¦46311.
\v 9 Then¦46313 \add @the presenter\add* must wash¦46315 the innards and the feet with¦46316 water¦46316, and the priest¦46318 must \add place it\add* on¦46316 the altar¦46322, \add ≈ensuring that every¦46321 part is completely burnt¦46323 up\add* as a pleasing¦46327 aroma¦46325 for¦46328 Yahweh¦46328.
\p
\v 10 “If¦46331 the offering¦46344 is from the flock¦46335 (i.e., a sheep¦46339 or¦46340 a goat), then it¦46347 must¦46347 be a healthy male¦46345 that's presented¦46347,
\v 11 and then¦46349 it¦46350 must be slaughtered¦46349 on the northern¦46354 side¦46352 of¦46352 the altar facing Yahweh¦46356, and Aharon's¦46359 sons¦46358, the priests¦46360, must splash its¦46363 blood¦46363 on the altar, all¦46367 around¦46367.
\v 12 Then¦46369 \add @the presenter\add* must cut¦46369 it¦46370 into¦46371 pieces¦46371 and they must arrange¦46378 them¦46380 (including¦46372 the head¦46374 and fat portions) on top of the burning¦46387 wood on the altar¦46391.
\v 13 Then¦46393 \add @the presenter¦46397\add* must wash¦46395 the innards and the feet with¦46396 water¦46396, and the priest¦46398 must \add place it\add* on¦46396 the altar¦46403, \add ≈ensuring that every¦46401 part is completely burnt¦46404 up\add* as a pleasing¦46408 aroma¦46407 for¦46409 Yahweh¦46409.
\p
\v 14 “\add ≈However,\add* if¦46412 his¦46417 burnt¦46416 offering¦46416 to¦46418 Yahweh¦46418 will¦46419 be a bird, then \add ≈it must¦46419 be either a dove¦46427 or¦46423 a young¦46426 pigeon¦46427\add*.
\v 15 The priest¦46433 must¦46432 present¦46432 it at the altar, and then¦46432 twist its¦46440 head off and burn¦46441 the head on the altar \add ≈as he\add* drains¦46443 the blood¦46444 out¦46443 onto the \add ≈outer edge\add* of¦46446 the altar.
\v 16 Then¦46449 he¦46449 must¦46449 \add ≈pull out\add* the crop with¦46453 its contents¦46453, and¦46449 throw¦46454 it¦46455 down on¦46453 the eastern¦46458 side of the altar¦46457 where the ashes¦46462 are.
\v 17 He¦46464 will¦46464 then¦46464 tear¦46464 the bird \add mostly\add* apart¦46464 by its¦46466 wings¦46466 \add ≈(but¦46464 making¦46469 sure that it stays as one piece)\add*, and¦46464 \add place it\add* on¦46466 the altar¦46472, \add ≈ensuring that every part is¦46480 completely burnt¦46480 up\add* as a pleasing¦46484 aroma¦46483 for¦46485 Yahweh¦46485.
\c 2
\s1 Procedures for grain offerings
\rem /s1 Procedures for the Grain Offering; The Grain Offering; Grain Offerings
\p
\v 1 “When someone¦46488 presents a grain¦46493 offering¦46493 to Yahweh¦46494, it must¦46491 be¦46496 as fine¦46495, wheat flour¦46495 \add ≈mixed with\add* \add olive\add*, and with frankincense¦46503 added to the top of it.
\v 2 It¦46505 must¦46505 be taken to the priest¦46510 Aharon's¦46509 sons¦46508 who will¦46511 take it¦46505 \add from them\add*, then¦46505 take a handful¦46514 out, being sure to get flour¦46515, oil¦46516, and some frankincense¦46520, and \add ≈throw that memorial¦46525 portion¦46525 onto\add* the altar¦46526 to get completely burnt up as a pleasing¦46529 aroma¦46528 for¦46530 Yahweh¦46530.
\v 3 Then¦46532 the rest of that grain¦46535 offering¦46535 will be for Aharon¦46536 and¦46537 his¦46537 sons¦46537—it's a very sacred¦46538 part from¦46540 the gifts for Yahweh¦46541.
\p
\v 4 “When¦46544 someone presents something baked as their grain¦46547 offering¦46547, it must¦46545 be baked without leaven: either wheat flour mixed¦46553 with \add olive\add* oil and¦46555 baked into flat buns, or else wheat flour biscuits¦46555 with \add olive\add* oil \add ≈spread on¦46554 top\add*.
\v 5 Or if¦46561 your¦46567 grain¦46563 offering¦46563 is¦46563 done on¦46570 the¦46566 flat pan, it¦46572 must¦46572 be¦46572 wheat flour¦46568 mixed¦46569 with¦46570 \add olive\add* oil¦46570 \add ≈and¦46561 made into flat biscuits\add*
\v 6 that should then be crumbled¦46574 and¦46577 have oil¦46579 poured¦46577 over them as \add &your\add* grain¦46580 offering¦46580.
\v 7 Or if¦46584 your¦46588 grain¦46586 offering¦46586 is¦46586 \add deep-fried\add* in¦46590 a pot, it¦46591 must¦46591 be¦46591 made¦46591 from wheat flour¦46589 with¦46590 \add olive\add* oil¦46590.
\p
\v 8 “\add Any of the above types of\add* grain¦46596 offering¦46596 to Yahweh¦46600 must¦46593 be presented¦46601 to the priest¦46604, and¦46593 he¦46605 will¦46605 take¦46605 it¦46605 to the altar¦46608.
\v 9 Then¦46610 he must¦46610 \add take a handful\add* as a memorial¦46617 portion¦46617 to the¦46619 altar¦46619 to get completely burnt up as a pleasing¦46622 aroma¦46621 for¦46623 Yahweh¦46623.
\v 10 Then¦46625 the rest of that grain¦46628 offering¦46628 will be for Aharon¦46629 and¦46630 his¦46630 sons¦46630—it's a very sacred¦46631 part from¦46633 the gifts for Yahweh¦46634.
\p
\v 11 “Anything that's presented¦46640 to Yahweh as a grain¦46638 offering¦46638 must¦46640 not¦46642 be made¦46643 with any rising agent, because¦46645 any leaven¦46648 or honey¦46651 mustn't be burnt as a gift to Yahweh.
\v 12 \add ≈Foods¦46663 containing those\add* can be presented as a first-fruits¦46660 offering, but¦46664 they¦46669 mustn't \add ≈be burnt\add* on the¦46666 altar¦46666.
\v 13 \add ≈However,\add* every¦46687 grain offering must be salted—the salt¦46681 in¦46677 the grain offering is a reminder of our agreement¦46682, \add ≈so it must always be present\add*.
\p
\v 14 “\add ≈Finally,\add* if¦46694 you¦46696 want to offer ripe grain as a grain offering to Yahweh¦46699, then it must¦46696 be a young¦46700 ear of grain that's been roasted¦46701 over the¦46707 fire¦46702 and¦46694 presented¦46705 as a grain offering of your¦46708 first¦46698 ripe grains
\v 15 \add ≈with\add* \add olive\add* oil¦46712 on it¦46717 and¦46713 some frankincense¦46715 on top. That \add also becomes\add* a grain¦46716 offering¦46716,
\v 16 and the priest¦46720 must¦46719 burn¦46719 a memorial¦46723 portion from it with some of the crushed¦46724 grains¦46724 and some of the oil¦46725 and some of the incense, \add to be burnt\add* to become smoke¦46719 as a gift to Yahweh¦46731.
\c 3
\s1 Procedures for peace offerings
\rem /s1 The Fellowship Offering; Fellowship Offerings; Offerings of Well-Being; Procedures for the Peace Offering
\p
\v 1 “\add ≈Now\add* if it's a sacrifice¦46736 for a peace¦46737 offering¦46737, if it's from the¦46742 \bd herd\bd*, then either a male¦46747 or¦46748 female¦46750 can be presented¦46744 to¦46753 Yahweh¦46754 \add ≈as long as it has no¦46751 defects¦46751\add*.
\v 2 \add @The presenter¦46761\add* must lay their hand¦46757 on the head¦46760 of the \add ≈bull or cow\add*, then¦46756 he must slaughter¦46762 it \add ≈in front of\add* the \add ≈sacred¦46764\add* tent¦46764. Then¦46756 the priest¦46769 Aharon's¦46768 sons¦46767 will¦46766 splash the blood¦46772 on the altar¦46775 \add ≈on each¦46776 side\add*.
\v 3 From that¦46794 peace¦46780 offering¦46781, he must¦46778 present¦46778 the fat¦46785 covering¦46786 the innards and¦46778 which¦46794 is part of the innards as a gift to Yahweh¦46782,
\v 4 \add ≈along with both¦46800\add* kidneys¦46801 and the fat¦46804 that's attached to them¦46806, and the lobe¦46813 on¦46814 the liver¦46816 (all of which need to be removed¦46820 \add from the animal\add*).
\v 5 Then¦46822 Aharon's¦46826 sons¦46824 must¦46822 burn¦46822 them on the wood \add ≈that's burning¦46838\add* on the altar¦46827 as a gift—to become smoke¦46822 as a pleasing¦46841 aroma¦46840 for¦46842 Yahweh¦46842.
\p
\v 6 “\add ≈However,\add* if¦46845 the sacrifice¦46851 for a peace¦46852 offering¦46850 is from the \bd flock¦46849\bd*, then either a male¦46854 or a female¦46856 can be presented¦46858 to Yahweh¦46853 \add ≈as long as it¦46858 has no¦46857 defects¦46857\add*.
\v 7 When the offering¦46868 is¦46865 being presented, it¦46870 must¦46869 be presented \add ≈in¦46871 front¦46871\add* of¦46871 Yahweh¦46872,
\v 8 and \add @the presenter¦46881\add* will¦46874 put his¦46877 hand¦46877 on the head¦46880 of \add the animal being\add* offered¦46881, and slaughter¦46882 it¦46883 \add ≈in¦46884 front¦46884\add* of the \add ≈sacred¦46885\add* tent¦46885. Then¦46874 Aharon's¦46889 sons¦46888 must¦46874 splash its¦46892 blood¦46892 on the altar¦46895, \add ≈on each¦46896 side\add*.
\v 9 From that¦46908 peace¦46900 offering¦46901, he must¦46898 present¦46898 the fat as a gift to Yahweh¦46902, \add ≈along with\add* the entire¦46917 fatty tail¦46904 (cut off near the backbone¦46907), the fat covering¦46912 the innards and¦46898 which¦46920 is¦46921 part of¦46917 the innards,
\v 10 \add ≈both¦46926\add* kidneys¦46927 and the fat¦46930 that's attached to them¦46932, and the lobe¦46939 on¦46940 the liver¦46942 (all of which need to be removed¦46946 \add from the animal\add*).
\v 11 Then¦46948 the priest¦46949 must¦46948 burn¦46948 them on the altar¦46950 as a food¦46951 offering¦46952 made¦46948 to¦46953 Yahweh¦46953 by fire¦46952.
\p
\v 12 “\add ≈Similarly,\add* if¦46956 the¦46960 offering¦46958 is¦46957 a \bd goat¦46957\bd*, then it¦46959 can be presented to¦46960 Yahweh¦46961.
\v 13 \add @The presenter\add* will¦46963 put his¦46966 hand¦46966 on its head¦46969, and¦46963 slaughter¦46970 it¦46971 \add ≈in¦46972 front¦46972\add* of the \add ≈sacred¦46973\add* tent¦46973. Then¦46963 Aharon's¦46977 sons¦46976 must¦46963 splash its blood¦46980 on the altar¦46983, \add ≈on each¦46984 side\add*.
\v 14 From that¦47002 peace offering¦46989, he must¦46986 present¦46986 the fat as a gift to Yahweh¦46990, \add ≈along with\add* the fat covering¦46994 the innards and¦46986 which¦47002 is part of the innards,
\v 15 \add ≈both¦47008\add* kidneys¦47009 and the fat¦47012 that's attached to them¦47014, and the lobe¦47021 on¦47022 the liver¦47024 (all of which need to be removed¦47028 \add from the animal\add*).
\v 16 Then¦47030 the priest¦47031 must¦47030 burn¦47030 them on the altar¦47032 as a food¦47033 offering¦47034 made¦47030 to Yahweh by fire¦47034 as a pleasing¦47036 aroma¦47035. All the fat¦47039 \add ≈belongs to\add* Yahweh.
\p
\v 17 “\add This\add* regulation¦47042 must be permanent¦47043 for \add all\add* \add ≈future\add* generations¦47044: All of you must \add ≈never\add* eat¦47054 fat¦47049 or blood¦47052.”
\c 4
\s1 Procedures for sin offerings
\rem /s1 Procedures for the Sin Offering; Offerings for Unintentional Sins; Sin Offerings; The Sin Offering
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦47058 told¦47062 Mosheh¦47061 \add !(Moses¦47061)\add*,
\v 2 “Tell¦47068 the \add ≈Israelis¦47067\add* that if¦47072 a person \add ≈breaks\add* any of Yahweh's¦47078 regulations unintentionally¦47075, or does anything that he said¦47070 shouldn't¦47080 be done¦47081, \add then this is the procedure:\add*
\p
\v 3 “If it's \bd the \+add ≈high\+add* priest¦47087\bd* \add ≈who's done it\add*, \add that causes\add* the \add ≈nation\add* to be guilty¦47090, and¦47092 for his¦47094 transgression he must¦47089 present¦47092 a bull¦47097 \add ≈with no¦47101 defects¦47101\add* to Yahweh¦47102 as a sin¦47103 offering¦47092.
\v 4 He¦47105 must bring¦47105 the bull¦47108 before Yahweh at the entrance¦47111 of the \add ≈sacred¦47112\add* tent¦47112, then¦47105 he¦47116 must place his¦47119 hand¦47119 on \add *its\add* head¦47122 and¦47105 slaughter¦47124 \add *it\add* \add ≈in¦47128 front¦47114\add* of Yahweh.
\v 5 Then¦47131 the \add ≈high\add* priest¦47132 must¦47131 get some¦47134 of the bull's¦47135 blood¦47134 and¦47131 bringing¦47136 it¦47137 into¦47138 the \add ≈sacred¦47140\add* tent¦47140,
\v 6 he must dip¦47143 his¦47147 finger¦47147 into \add *it\add* and¦47143 seven¦47153 times¦47154 he must \add ≈flick\add* the blood¦47152 \add ≈towards\add* the \add ≈front\add* of the curtain¦47160 of the sanctuary¦47161 \add ≈in¦47148 front\add* of Yahweh¦47156.
\v 7 Then the \add high\add* priest¦47164 must put some¦47165 of the blood on¦47177 the \add four\add* \add ≈projecting corners\add* of the incense altar (\add ≈located\add* inside the sacred tent \add ≈in¦47177 front¦47174\add* of Yahweh¦47175 \add and\add* used for \add burning\add* fragrant¦47173 incense). Then \add he must go back outside and\add* pour¦47185 \add ≈the rest\add* of the bull's¦47184 blood out at the base¦47188 of the altar for burnt¦47190 offerings¦47190 (which¦47191 is¦47193 \add out\add* at the entrance¦47193 to the \add ≈sacred\add* tent).
\v 8 Then¦47197 \add @the high priest\add* must¦47204 \add ≈remove\add* all¦47199 the fat from the bull¦47202 being offered as a sin¦47203 offering¦47203, \add including\add* the fat covering¦47209 the innards and¦47213 which¦47217 is a part of the innards,
\v 9 \add ≈both¦47223\add* kidneys¦47224 and the fat¦47227 that's attached to them¦47229, and the lobe¦47236 on¦47237 the liver¦47239 (all of which need to be removed¦47243 \add from the animal\add*)
\v 10 just \add ≈like you'd do\add* for the bull¦47247 of¦47247 the peace¦47249 offering. Then the priest¦47251 must¦47250 burn¦47250 them¦47250 on¦47252 the altar¦47253 for the burnt¦47254 offering.
\v 11 \add ≈However,\add* the bull's¦47259 hide¦47258 and its meat¦47264, plus¦47260 it's head¦47267 and feet, and innards and dung¦47272,
\v 12 must be taken outside¦47282 the camp¦47283 to the clean place¦47286 where they pour out¦47290 the ashes, and they must \add make\add* a fire¦47297 with¦47297 wood there on¦47297 top of¦47300 the ashes and burn¦47292 it¦47302 there.
\p
\v 13 “If¦47309 it's the \bd whole¦47306 community\bd* that \add unintentionally\add* transgresses by doing something that Yahweh¦47320 prohibited, then they'll¦47310 be guilty¦47325 even if the community didn't¦47322 \add actually\add* notice¦47313.\x + \xo 4:13-21: \xt Num 15:22-26.\x*
\v 14 \add ≈When¦47327\add* they become aware of \add ≈what they've¦47330 done wrong\add*, then¦47327 the community must¦47327 present¦47332 a \add ≈young¦47335\add* bull¦47334 \add ≈in¦47341 front¦47341\add* of the \add ≈sacred¦47342\add* tent¦47342 as a sin offering¦47332.
\v 15 Then¦47345 the community¦47347 elders¦47346 must¦47345 place their¦47350 hands¦47350 of the bull's¦47354 head¦47353 \add ≈in front¦47355\add* of Yahweh¦47356, and¦47357 slaughter¦47357 \add *it there\add*.
\v 16 The \add ≈high\add* priest¦47365 must¦47364 take some¦47367 of the bull's¦47368 blood¦47367 into¦47369 the \add ≈sacred¦47371\add* tent¦47371
\v 17 and seven¦47381 times¦47382 \add *he¦47374\add* must¦47374 dip¦47374 his¦47376 finger¦47376 in it and \add ≈flick\add* the blood¦47379 \add ≈towards\add* the \add ≈front\add* of the curtain¦47387 of the sanctuary \add ≈in front\add* of Yahweh¦47384.
\v 18 Then he must put¦47392 some of the blood on¦47402 the \add four\add* \add ≈projecting corners\add* of the \add incense\add* altar (\add ≈located\add* inside the sacred tent \add ≈in¦47402 front¦47399\add* of Yahweh¦47400). Then \add he must go back outside and\add* pour¦47408 \add ≈the rest of\add* the bull's blood out at the base¦47411 of the altar for burnt¦47413 offerings¦47413 (which¦47414 is¦47416 \add out\add* at the entrance¦47416 to the \add ≈sacred\add* tent).
\v 19 Then¦47420 he must¦47424 \add ≈remove\add* all¦47421 the fat from \add @the slaughtered bull\add* and¦47420 burn¦47426 up \add @that fat\add* on the altar¦47427,
\v 20 and he should process the bull just the same as for a sin¦47434 offering¦47434. Then¦47429 the priest¦47441 \add ≈will have made¦47439 them¦47440 right with God\add* and they'll be¦47442 forgiven¦47442.
\v 21 Then¦47445 he¦47445 must¦47445 take \add the remaining parts of\add* the bull¦47448 outside¦47451 the camp¦47452 and burn¦47453 it \add ≈as¦47455 described above\add* \add and\add* it will be the sin¦47460 offering¦47460 for the community.
\p
\v 22 “If it's \bd a leader¦47466\bd* that \add unintentionally\add* transgresses by doing something that his god¦47474 Yahweh¦47473 prohibited, then he'll¦47467 be guilty¦47480.
\v 23 \add Alternatively, maybe someone else will point out his failing\add* to¦47485 him¦47485. Then he must¦47490 bring¦47490 an offering¦47493 of a male goat¦47494 \add ≈with¦47489 no¦47497 defects¦47497\add*.
\v 24 He¦47499 must¦47499 place his¦47500 hand¦47500 on¦47507 \add *its¦47500\add* head¦47503 and¦47499 slaughter it¦47506 in¦47507 the place where¦47508 \add the animals for\add* the burnt¦47513 offering are slaughtered. It¦47515 will be a sin¦47516 offering,
\v 25 and the priest¦47520 must¦47519 take¦47519 some of the blood of that sin¦47522 offering and with¦47523 his finger¦47523, put some of the blood on¦47523 the \add four\add* \add ≈projecting corners\add* of the altar¦47537 for burnt¦47538 offerings¦47538.
\v 26 He must¦47545 burn up all¦47542 the fat on the altar¦47546 just like¦47547 the fat from the peace¦47549 offering¦47549, and \add that way\add*, the priest¦47552 will make¦47550 \add @that leader\add* \add ≈right with God\add* and he'll¦47545 be¦47554 forgiven¦47554.
\p
\v 27 “If¦47558 it's¦47562 \bd an \+add ordinary\+add* person¦47560\bd* who \add unintentionally\add* transgresses by¦47563 doing¦47566 something that¦47570 Yahweh¦47569 prohibited, then they'll¦47573 be guilty¦47574.\x + \xo 4:27-31: \xt Num 15:27-28.\x*
\v 28 \add Alternatively, maybe someone else will point out their failing\add* to them. Then due to their transgression, \add @that¦47580 person\add* must¦47582 bring¦47582 an offering¦47583 of a female goat¦47584 \add ≈with no¦47586 defects¦47586\add*.
\v 29 He¦47594 must¦47594 place his¦47597 hand¦47597 on¦47605 the head¦47599 of the \add goat that's a\add* sin offering, and¦47594 slaughter¦47601 \add *it\add* at¦47605 the place for burnt¦47606 offerings¦47606.
\v 30 Then¦47608 the priest¦47609 must¦47608 take¦47608 some of the blood \add and\add* with¦47611 his finger¦47611, put some of the blood on¦47611 the \add four\add* \add ≈projecting corners\add* of the altar for burnt¦47617 offerings¦47617, and then¦47608 pour¦47623 \add the rest of\add* the blood at¦47611 the altar's base.
\v 31 Then¦47629 \add @the presenter\add* must¦47634 pull out all¦47631 the fat¦47633 (just¦47635 like it's¦47636 done for the peace¦47640 offering¦47640), and the priest¦47642 will \add ≈burn¦47641\add* it on the altar¦47643 to make a pleasing¦47645 aroma¦47644 for Yahweh¦47646, and to \add ≈make that person right with God\add*, and they'll be¦47650 forgiven¦47650.
\p
\v 32 “\add ≈But¦47654\add* if¦47654 it's a lamb¦47656 that \add @the individual\add* wants to¦47659 bring¦47662 as their sin¦47659 offering¦47659, then it must¦47657 be a female¦47660 \add ≈with no¦47661 defects¦47661\add*.
\v 33 \add @The presenter\add* must¦47664 put their hand¦47667 on¦47674 the head¦47669 of the \add ≈lamb\add* and¦47664 slaughter it¦47672 as a sin¦47673 offering¦47673 at¦47674 the place¦47674 where¦47675 burnt¦47679 offerings¦47679 are slaughtered.
\v 34 Then¦47681 the priest¦47682 must¦47681 take¦47681 some of the blood \add and\add* with¦47685 his finger¦47685, put some of the blood on¦47685 the \add four\add* \add ≈projecting corners\add* of the altar for burnt¦47691 offerings¦47691, and then¦47681 pour¦47697 \add the rest of\add* the blood at¦47685 the altar's base.
\v 35 Then¦47703 \add @the presenter\add* must¦47708 pull out all¦47705 the fat¦47707 (just¦47709 like it's¦47710 done for the peace¦47715 offering¦47721), and the priest¦47717 will \add ≈burn¦47716\add* it on the altar¦47719 to make a pleasing aroma for Yahweh¦47722, and to \add ≈make that¦47729 person right with God\add*, and they'll be¦47732 forgiven¦47732.
\c 5
\s1 More needs for sin offerings
\rem /s1 Cases Requiring Sin Offerings; Sins Requiring a Sin Offering
\p
\v 1 “If¦47737 someone¦47736 is a witness¦47744 to a case \add ≈because¦47737 of¦47741 something they saw¦47746 or¦47747 knew¦47748 about¦47741\add*, and¦47753 hears¦47740 an¦47742 oath¦47742 \add in court\add* but¦47736 doesn't \add ≈testify\add*, then they'll \add ≈become guilty¦47754\add*.
\p
\v 2 “If someone¦47757 touches¦47759 anything ‘unclean’ (e.g., a dead domestic or wild animal¦47766, or some creature) \add ≈without realising it\add*, they \add still\add* become ‘unclean’ and is guilty¦47781.
\p
\v 3 “If¦47784 someone touches¦47785 anything ‘unclean¦47791’ from¦47794 \add another\add* person, even if¦47784 they \add initially\add* \add ≈didn't realise what it¦47794 was\add*, they become guilty¦47797.
\p
\v 4 “If¦47801 someone¦47800 makes a rash promise, whether it's¦47802 to¦47805 do¦47805 something good¦47808 or¦47807 something bad, and even if they were \add just\add* speaking¦47811 carelessly but¦47814 find out \add later\add*, they become guilty¦47819.
\p
\v 5 “\add ≈So¦47823 then¦47823\add* \add @the person\add* that became guilty¦47826 from¦47828 any of the above, must¦47826 confess¦47829 their wrongdoing
\v 6 and present a penalty¦47837 to Yahweh¦47838 to address that¦47841 wrongdoing, and must¦47834 present a female¦47843 lamb¦47847 or¦47848 young goat¦47850 as a sin¦47852 offering¦47852, and the priest¦47855 will¦47853 \add act on their behalf\add* \add ≈to make¦47853 them right with God\add*.
\p
\v 7 “\add ≈However if¦47858 that¦47870 person can't¦47860 afford\add* a sheep¦47865, then¦47858 for their guilt¦47869 offering because of¦47869 \add ≈their transgression\add*, they must¦47861 bring¦47866 two¦47872 doves or¦47874 \add ≈young¦47877\add* pigeons to¦47880 Yahweh¦47880—one for a sin¦47882 offering and¦47858 one for a burnt¦47884 offering.
\v 8 \add @The presenter¦47891\add* must¦47886 take them¦47887 to¦47888 the priest¦47890 who will¦47891 present¦47891 the sin¦47895 offering¦47891 first¦47896—twisting \add @the bird's\add* head¦47900 off¦47897 from¦47901 the neck¦47902, \add ≈but¦47886\add* not \add ≈pulling\add* the body \add in half\add*.
\v 9 Then¦47906 he¦47906 must¦47906 sprinkle¦47906 some¦47907 of the blood from that sin offering onto the side¦47911 of the altar, and¦47906 then¦47906 drain¦47915 out the rest of the blood at¦47914 the altar's base. That will be the sin offering.
\v 10 The second¦47925 \add >bird\add* will be for a burnt¦47927 offering¦47926 as per the regulation¦47928. The priest¦47931 will make¦47929 \add @the presenter\add* \add ≈right with God\add* after their wrongdoing, and¦47923 they'll be forgiven¦47936.
\p
\v 11 “\add ≈However if¦47940 that¦47956 person still can't¦47942 afford\add* those two¦47945 doves or \add ≈young¦47949\add* pigeons, then they can present¦47955 a kilogram of fine¦47960 wheat flour¦47960 for a sin offering. It mustn't have¦47957 oil¦47966 or frankincense¦47971 in it because¦47972 it's a sin offering.
\v 12 \add @The presenter\add* must¦47976 take it to the priest¦47979 who will then¦47976 take a handful¦47980 of it, and¦47976 burn¦47989 that memorial¦47988 portion¦47988 on the altar¦47990 for burnt offerings¦47992 to Yahweh¦47993. That will be the sin¦47994 offering¦47994.
\v 13 The priest will make¦47997 \add @the presenter\add* \add ≈right with God\add* after their wrongdoing as per one of the above¦47998, and¦47997 they'll be forgiven¦48008. \add The rest of\add* \add @the flour\add* will be for the priest, similar to a grain¦48012 offering¦48012.”
\s1 More on guilt offerings
\rem /s1 Repayment Offerings; The Guilt Offering; Procedures for the Guilt Offering; Offerings with Restitution
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh¦48016 told¦48020 Mosheh¦48019 \add !(Moses¦48019)\add*,
\v 15 “If¦48035 someone acts¦48025 unfaithfully¦48025 \add ≈but¦48027\add* unintentionally¦48028 \add ≈desecrates something that's sacred¦48029\add* to Yahweh¦48030, then because¦48023 of their guilt, \add @that person\add* must¦48025 present ram¦48036 \add ≈with¦48028 no¦48037 defects¦48037\add* from¦48038 the flock¦48040, or silver¦48042 \add ≈coins using the official valuation¦48041\add*, as a guilt offering¦48048.
\v 16 They must also compensate for the sacred¦48055 item, but¦48050 with an extra twenty¦48059 percent¦48060. That must all be given¦48062 to¦48064 the priest¦48065 who will¦48066 make¦48066 \add @the presenter\add* \add ≈right with God\add* with that¦48051 ram¦48068 as the guilt¦48069 offering¦48069, and¦48050 they'll be forgiven¦48070.
\p
\v 17 “If¦48074 someone¦48076 transgresses by violating¦48079 \add even\add* one¦48080 of¦48083 Yahweh's¦48084 \add ≈regulations or prohibitions\add*, even if¦48077 they didn't¦48086 realise it¦48078, they become guilty¦48091 and must¦48078 bear¦48092 responsibility for that¦48085 guilt¦48091.
\v 18 Then¦48095 \add @that person\add* must¦48095 take a ram¦48096 from¦48098 the flock¦48100 that \add ≈has¦48113 no¦48097 defects¦48097\add* to the priest for a guilt¦48102 offering¦48102. Then the priest will¦48118 make¦48106 \add @the presenter\add* \add ≈right with¦48101 God\add* after that transgression (even if they weren't aware of¦48110 it¦48118), and¦48095 they'll be¦48118 forgiven¦48118.
\v 19 That's a guilt¦48121 offering¦48121, because they were clearly guilty before Yahweh¦48125.”
\c 6
\rem /s1 Sins Requiring a Guilt Offering
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦48271 told¦48275 Mosheh¦48274 \add !(Moses)\add*,\x + \xo 6:1-7: \xt Num 5:5-8.\x*
\v 2 “Someone who transgresses against Yahweh by \add ≈cheating\add* with respect to¦48285 a deposit or a pledge, or by theft or extortion,
\v 3 or by finding lost property and then denying it¦48324, or making an oath that was knowingly false, or by any other typical transgression that people do,
\v 4 then they've become guilty as a result of¦48340 that transgression. \add @That person\add* must¦48337 return what was stolen or extorted, or \add ≡return\add* the deposit or the lost property,
\v 5 or \add ≈make¦48376 amends for a false oath\add*. They must¦48362 return the full value plus¦48358 an extra twenty percent \add ≈as soon as the guilt is discovered\add*.
\v 6 Then \add @that person\add* must¦48384 take a ram from the flock \add ≈with no defects\add* to the priest as a guilt offering to Yahweh,
\v 7 and the priest will¦48397 make \add @the presenter¦48397\add* \add ≈right with Yahweh¦48403\add* after whatever that¦48394 transgression was to¦48404 make them guilty, and they'll¦48397 be forgiven.
\s1 The fire and the ashes
\rem /s1 Further Instructions for the Burnt Offering; Sacrifices Burned Whole; The Burnt Offering; Instructions concerning Sacrifices
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh¦48429 told Mosheh,
\v 9 “Tell¦48435 Aharon¦48435 \add !(Aaron¦48435)\add* and his sons¦48436 that these are the \add #regulations\add* for the burnt \add #offerings\add*: The burnt offering must remain¦48432 on the altar's hearth, on the altar all night until the morning and the fire \add ≈must be kept burning\add* on it¦48445.
\v 10 Then¦48452 the priest must¦48449 \add ≈put on\add* his linen underwear and linen robe and \add ≈remove\add* the fatty ashes from¦48454 the burnt \add #offerings¦48454\add* on the altar, and pile them beside \add *it\add*.
\v 11 \add ≈After that\add*, he'll¦48467 \add ≈get changed again into his regular\add* clothes and take \add &those\add* ashes outside the¦48465 camp to¦48471 a ‘clean’ place.
\v 12 \add ≈Meanwhile,\add* the fire on the altar must be kept burning—it must never go out. Every morning the priest must put more wood on the fire, then he must arrange more offerings on the fire—he should burn the fat of the fellowship offerings on that fire so the smoke rises.
\v 13 \add Yes,\add* the fire must¦48497 burn continually¦48506 on¦48508 the altar—never going out.
\s1 Procedures for the grain offering
\rem /s1 Further Instructions for the Grain Offering; The Grain Offering; Grain Offerings
\p
\v 14 “Here's the regulations for the grain¦48521 offering¦48521: Aharon's sons must¦48517 present¦48523 it \add ≈before\add* Yahweh¦48527 \add ≈in¦48516 front\add* of the altar.
\v 15 \add @The priest¦48530\add* must¦48534 take a handful of the flour from that grain offering¦48534, being sure it includes some of the oil and the incense, and burn it on the altar as a memorial portion¦48536 to become a pleasing aroma for Yahweh¦48539.
\v 16 The remainder \add of that flour\add* will be \add ≈used as food\add* for Aharon and¦48544 his sons—they must¦48549 eat¦48551 the flat bread in a sacred place, i.e., in the courtyard of the \add ≈sacred\add* tent.
\v 17 It must not be baked with any rising agent. I give that to them from \add ≈what's given to me\add*. It's \add ≈very\add* sacred just like the sin offering and¦48555 the guilt offering.
\v 18 Any of Aharon's¦48566 male descendants can eat it—it'll¦48578 be their portion from Yahweh's gifts throughout the generations. Anyone who touches \add @Yahweh's gifts\add* will¦48578 become sacred¦48582 \add themself\add*.”
\s1 Procedures for the ordination offering
\rem /s1 Procedures for the Ordination Offering
\p
\v 19 Then Yahweh told Mosheh,
\v 20 “On the day when one of Aharon's male descendants is consecrated as a priest, this is what he¦48606 must¦48602 present to Yahweh: a continual grain offering of a kilogram of fine wheat flour—half for the morning and¦48605 half for the evening.
\v 21 It must be mixed with \add olive\add* oil then cooked with oil on¦48629 a flat pan. It must then be broken¦48626 into pieces and presented as a grain offering to make a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 22 The priest who's next in¦48640 line to become \add ≈high\add* priest must offer it and it must be completely burnt for Yahweh. This is a permanent regulation.
\v 23 Any grain offering¦48650 by a priest must be completely burnt¦48663—it must not be eaten¦48661.”
\s1 Procedures for the sin offering
\rem /s1 Sin Offerings; Further Instructions for the Sin Offering; The Sin Offering
\p
\v 24 Then Yahweh told Mosheh,
\v 25 “Tell Aharon and his sons that these are the \add #regulations\add* for the sin \add #offerings\add*: The sin offering must be slaughtered \add ≈in front\add* of Yahweh at the place where the burnt \add #offerings\add* are slaughtered. It's a \add ≈very\add* sacred \add activity\add*.
\v 26 The priest who offers the sin offering \add ≈must be the one to eat it\add*. It must be eaten in a sacred place, i.e., in the courtyard of the \add ≈sacred\add* tent.
\v 27 Everything that touches the \add animal's\add* body will become sacred. Anyone who \add ≈gets the blood on their\add* clothes, must wash them in a sacred place.
\v 28 Any clay pot that it's been boiled in, must then be shattered, but if it's boiled in a bronze pot, then that must be scoured and rinsed with water.
\v 29 Any male in the priests' \add families\add* may eat it—it's \add ≈very\add* sacred.
\v 30 \add ≈However,\add* every sin offering that has its blood taken inside the \add ≈sacred\add* tent to make \add ≈someone right with God\add*, then that \add meat\add* mustn't be eaten—it must be completely burnt up.
\c 7
\s1 Procedures for the guilt offering
\rem /s1 Repayment Offerings; The Guilt Offering; Further Instructions for the Guilt Offering
\p
\v 1 “Then these are the \add #regulations\add* for the \add ≈very\add* sacred¦48669 guilt¦48668 \add #offerings¦48668\add*:
\v 2 The guilt¦48682 offering¦48678 must be slaughtered at the same place¦48673 as the burnt¦48678 \add #offerings¦48682\add*, and¦48683 the blood¦48685 must be splashed on \add ≈each¦48690 side of\add* the altar¦48689.
\v 3 \add @The presenter¦48696\add* must¦48696 present¦48696 all¦48693 the fat \add including\add* the fatty tail¦48699, the fat covering¦48703 the innards,
\v 4 \add ≈both¦48709\add* kidneys¦48710 and the fat¦48713 that's attached to them¦48715, and the lobe¦48722 on¦48723 the liver¦48725 (\add ≈all of which\add* need to be removed¦48729 \add from the animal\add*).
\v 5 Then¦48731 the priest¦48733 must¦48731 burn¦48731 those on the altar¦48734 as a guilt¦48737 offering¦48735 to¦48736 Yahweh¦48736.
\v 6 Any male¦48742 in¦48743 the priests' \add families\add* may eat¦48744 it—it's \add ≈very\add* sacred¦48746.
\v 7 The regulations are the same for the guilt offering as¦48753 for the sin¦48752 offering—the priest¦48757 who¦48758 \add ≈offers the guilt offering\add* \add ≈must¦48759 be the one to¦48762 eat it\add*.
\p
\v 8 “Whenever a priest presents¦48766 someone's burnt¦48769 offering¦48769, the skin¦48771 of¦48771 the animal that was presented¦48774 belongs¦48777 to¦48775 that priest.
\v 9 Any grain¦48781 offering¦48781 that's baked¦48783 in an oven¦48784, or cooked in a pan¦48788 or on¦48784 a griddle, will belong¦48796 to the priest¦48792 who¦48793 presents¦48793 it.
\v 10 Any¦48805 grain¦48800 offering¦48800 that's either dry¦48804 or mixed¦48801 with¦48803 oil¦48803, belongs¦48809 to¦48805 all¦48805 of¦48807 Aharon's¦48808 male descendants \add ≈without any¦48805 distinction\add*.
\s1 Procedures for the peace offering
\rem /s1 The Fellowship Offering; Further Instructions; Fellowship Offerings; Further Instructions for the Peace Offering
\p
\v 11 “Then these are the \add #regulations\add* for sacrificing¦48816 peace¦48817 offerings¦48817 to present¦48819 to Yahweh¦48820:
\v 12 If it's¦48826 presented¦48827 \add ≈from thankfulness\add*, then the sacrifice¦48831 \add ≈must¦48826 be accompanied by\add* thanksgiving¦48825 flat bread¦48834 buns (\add made from flour\add* mixed with \add olive\add* oil), and flat biscuits¦48837 smeared¦48839 with oil, and buns \add made from\add* fine¦48841 wheat flour mixed with oil,
\v 13 \add ≈plus\add* risen bread¦48850 rolls. \add All that\add* must¦48852 accompany the peace¦48858 offering¦48853 \add ≈from thankfulness¦48857\add*.
\v 14 Then¦48860 he¦48860 must¦48860 present¦48865 one¦48862 of each¦48863 \add kind of bread\add* as a contribution¦48866 to Yahweh¦48867. Those will belong¦48875 to the priest¦48868 who¦48869 splashes the blood¦48872 for the peace¦48873 offerings¦48873.
\v 15 The¦48877 meat¦48877 of the thankfulness¦48879 sacrifice¦48878 of those peace¦48880 offerings¦48880 must¦48883 be eaten¦48883 the \add ≈same day¦48881 that it's offered—none of can be left¦48886\add* until¦48888 morning¦48890.
\v 16 \add ≈However,\add* if¦48892 the sacrificed¦48898 offering is¦48894 a vow¦48894 or¦48896 freewill¦48897 offering, then¦48892 it¦48905 can be¦48905 eaten¦48905 that same day and¦48892 \add also\add* the next¦48906 day,
\v 17 \add ≈but¦48911\add* any left-over meat¦48912 must¦48917 be burnt¦48917 up on the fire¦48916 on the third¦48915 day¦48914.
\v 18 If¦48919 any of that meat¦48922 from the sacrifice¦48924 was eaten on the third¦48927 day¦48926, the presenter¦48930 won't be accepted¦48929 and \add ≈they won't be forgiven\add*. It is spoilt, and anyone¦48930 who¦48938 eats¦48938 any of it¦48941 will¦48941 bear¦48941 their own \add ≈guilt\add*.
\v 19 If any meat touches¦48946 anything that's ‘unclean¦48949’, then¦48943 it must not¦48950 be eaten—it must be burnt¦48953 up in¦48952 the fire¦48952. \add ≈However,\add* any long as the meat remains ‘clean’, it can be eaten by any person who's ‘clean’.
\v 20 If¦48969 a person who¦48962 was ‘unclean¦48970’ were to¦48969 eat¦48964 any of¦48970 the meat¦48965 from the sacrifice¦48966 for¦48969 a peace¦48967 offering¦48967, then¦48961 that¦48968 \add >person\add* must¦48964 be¦48972 cut¦48972 off¦48972 from \add ≈the community\add*.
\v 21 \add ≈Similarly,\add* when a person touches¦48980 anything ‘unclean’ (e.g., human¦48985 uncleanness¦48984, or an unclean animal¦48988, or a detestable¦48993 creature¦49003), and¦48977 then¦48977 they eat¦48995 some of the meat¦48996 of the sacrifice¦48998 for a peace¦48999 offering¦48999 that \add now\add* belongs to Yahweh¦49001, that \add >person\add* must¦48980 be¦49002 cut¦49002 off¦49002 from¦49005 \add ≈the community\add*.
\s1 Don't eat fat or blood
\rem /s1 Eating Fat and Blood Forbidden; The Forbidden Blood and Fat
\p
\v 22 Then Yahweh¦49009 told¦49013 Mosheh¦49012,
\v 23 “Tell¦49019 the \add ≈Israelis¦49018\add* that you all must not eat¦49028 the fat¦49023 from \add #cattle or sheep¦49025 or goats\add*,
\v 24 ⇔plus¦49032 you all¦49035 definitely must¦49034 not¦49039 eat¦49038 the fat from an animal¦49033 \add ≈that died naturally or was killed by wild animals although you can use it for other purposes\add*.
\v 25 Anyone¦49045 who¦49045 does eat the fat¦49046 from an animal presented¦49051 as a gift to¦49054 Yahweh¦49054 must¦49051 be¦49055 cut¦49055 off¦49055 from \add ≈the community\add*.
\p
\v 26 “\add ≈Also¦49060,\add* you all¦49060 must not eat¦49064 blood¦49062 from either a bird or an animal¦49068, \add ≈no matter where you're living¦49066\add*.\x + \xo 7:26-27: \xt Gen 9:4; Lev 17:10-14; 19:26; Deu 12:16,23; 15:23.\x*
\v 27 Any person¦49072 who does eat¦49075 blood¦49078 must¦49075 be¦49079 cut¦49079 off¦49079 from \add ≈the community\add*.”
\s1 The priests' portions
\rem /s1 The Priests' Share; A Portion for the Priests
\p
\v 28 Then Yahweh¦49086 told¦49090 Mosheh¦49089,
\v 29 “Tell the \add ≈Israelis¦49095\add* that anyone¦49098 who¦49098 offers a peace offering to Yahweh must¦49104 take \add ≈some of¦49107 it as\add* his¦49107 peace offering to Yahweh.
\v 30 They¦49113 must¦49113 \add ≈personally carry\add* Yahweh's¦49116 gift \add which will be\add* the breast with its fat and raise it \add ≈in¦49129 front¦49129\add* of¦49129 Yahweh as a wave¦49126 offering¦49115,
\v 31 and the priest¦49133 must¦49132 burn¦49132 that fat¦49136 up on the altar¦49137 \add ≈but¦49132\add* the breast¦49139 will be for Aharon¦49140 and his¦49141 sons¦49141.
\v 32 The right¦49145 thigh¦49144 from the sacrifices¦49149 of your peace¦49150 offerings¦49150 must¦49146 be given¦49146 to the priest¦49148—
\v 33 whichever \add ≈priest (Aharon's¦49161 descendant) who¦49152 handled\add* the blood¦49155 of¦49155 the peace¦49156 offerings¦49152 and¦49157 the fat¦49159, that right¦49165 thigh¦49164 will¦49163 be his portion¦49166
\v 34 because¦49168 I took¦49177 the breast¦49171 from the wave offering¦49183 and the thigh¦49175 from the contribution¦49176 of¦49179 the \add ≈Israelis'\add* peace¦49183 offerings¦49183, and I gave¦49184 them¦49185 to¦49186 Aharon¦49186 the priest¦49187 and his¦49188 sons¦49188 as an ongoing portion¦49189 from the \add ≈Israeli¦49179 people¦49193\add*.
\v 35 That's the share¦49197 for Aharon¦49198 and¦49199 his sons¦49200 from¦49201 the gifts to Yahweh since Mosheh presented¦49204 them¦49205 to Yahweh as¦49206 priests¦49206.
\v 36 Yahweh¦49211 commanded¦49210 them to be given¦49212 to them \add back\add* on¦49214 the day¦49214 when he¦49215 anointed¦49215 them out of all the \add ≈Israeli¦49218 people¦49218\add* \add and made it\add* a permanent¦49221 regulation for \add future\add* generations¦49222.
\p
\v 37 \add #Those are the regulations\add* for the burnt¦49226 \add #offerings\add*, the grain¦49227 \add #offerings\add*, the sin¦49228 \add #offerings\add*, the guilt¦49229 \add #offerings\add*, the ordination¦49230 \add #offerings\add*, and¦49231 the peace¦49232 \add #offerings\add*
\v 38 that Yahweh \add ≈gave to\add* Mosheh¦49239 at¦49240 Mt¦49240. Sinai on¦49240 the day¦49242 when he¦49243 ordered¦49235 the \add ≈Israelis¦49246\add* to present¦49248 their¦49251 offerings¦49251 to Yahweh \add when they were there\add* in¦49240 the Sinai wilderness¦49253.
\c 8
\s1 Ordination of Aharon and his sons
\rem /s1 The Rites of Ordination; Ordination of the Priests; The priesthood; The Ordination of Aaron and His Sons
\r (Exo. 29:1-37)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦49258 told¦49262 Mosheh¦49261 \add !(Moses¦49261)\add*,
\v 2 “Take¦49267 Aharon¦49267 \add !(Aaron¦49267)\add* and his¦49270 sons¦49270 with¦49271 their clothes¦49273 and the anointing¦49276 oil¦49275, and the bull¦49279 for the sin¦49280 offering¦49280 and two¦49282 rams¦49283, and a basket¦49285 of unleavened¦49286 bread¦49286,
\v 3 and¦49288 assemble¦49292 the entire¦49289 \add ≈community\add* at the entrance¦49295 to the \add ≈sacred¦49296\add* tent¦49296.”
\p
\v 4 \add ≈So¦49299\add* Mosheh¦49300 did exactly has Yahweh¦49303 had¦49302 commanded¦49302 him¦49304, and¦49305 the \add ≈people\add* assembled¦49305 at the entrance¦49309 of¦49309 the \add ≈sacred¦49310\add* tent¦49310.
\v 5 Then¦49313 Mosheh¦49314 told the¦49317 \add ≈assembled people\add*, “This¦49318 \add ceremony\add* is¦49319 what Yahweh¦49323 has¦49322 told us to¦49315 do¦49324.”
\p
\v 6 Then¦49326 Mosheh¦49327 presented¦49326 Aharon¦49330 and his¦49333 sons¦49333 and \add after\add* washing¦49334 them¦49335 with¦49336 water¦49336,
\v 7 he put the tunic¦49342 on \add @Aharon\add* and wrapped the sash¦49345 \add around\add* him, and then¦49338 put the robe¦49350 on him. Then¦49338 he put the sacred apron on him, tying \add *its\add* finely woven waistband to fasten it onto him.
\v 8 Then¦49364 he¦49364 \add ≈attached\add* the chest pouch and put the Urim¦49375 and Tummim¦49378 into the pouch.
\v 9 \add ≈Finally\add* he¦49380 placed the turban on¦49384 his¦49386 head¦49386, and¦49380 fastened the golden¦49397 flower-shaped plate¦49396 (that sacred¦49399 crown¦49398 that demonstrates that Aharon is set apart to only serve Yahweh) to the front¦49393 of the turban, exactly as¦49400 Yahweh had¦49401 instructed¦49401 Mosheh¦49405.
\p
\v 10 Then¦49407 Mosheh¦49408 took¦49407 the anointing¦49412 oil¦49411 and anointed¦49413 \add &Yahweh's\add* residence and everything in¦49423 it, thus consecrating¦49424 \add ≈it all\add*.
\v 11 Seven times¦49433 he¦49427 flicked some of it¦49428 onto the altar—anointing¦49434 the altar and all¦49440 its utensils¦49442 and the basin and its base¦49448, thus consecrating them¦49449.
\v 12 He¦49451 poured¦49451 some¦49452 of the anointing oil¦49452 over Aharon's¦49456 head¦49455, thus anointing him to¦49459 consecrate him.
\v 13 Then¦49461 Mosheh presented¦49461 Aharon's¦49466 sons¦49465 and dressed them in tunics¦49468 and wrapped sashes around each of them, and tied¦49472 their headbands onto them, just¦49475 as¦49475 Yahweh¦49477 had¦49476 instructed¦49476 Mosheh.
\p
\v 14 Then¦49482 he¦49482 brought¦49482 the bull¦49484 for the sin¦49485 offering¦49485, and Aharon¦49487 and his¦49488 sons¦49488 placed their¦49491 hands¦49491 on¦49492 \add *its\add* head¦49494.
\v 15 Then \add ?@Aharon\add*\f + \fr 8:15 \ft It's not 100% clear from the Hebrew if the pronoun here refers to Aharon or to Mosheh, but as Mosheh's name is specifically mentioned in the next clause, we assume that ‘he’ was still referring to Aharon here.\f* slaughtered it, and Mosheh¦49500 took¦49499 the blood and used his finger¦49510 to put some on¦49505 the \add four\add* \add ≈projecting corners\add* of the altar, thus purifying the altar. Then he¦49518 poured¦49518 \add the rest of\add* the blood out at the altar's¦49522 base¦49521, sanctifying it \add ≈so it could be used for making¦49524 people right with God\add*.
