\v 47 If a foreigner living among you¦59643 \add ≈becomes wealthy\add*, \add ≈yet\add* your \add ≈fellow citizen\add* becomes poor and¦59647 sells¦59647 himself¦59647 to that resident¦59649 foreigner or¦59651 to a member¦59652 of their clan¦59653,
\v 48 then there's always the right¦59658 of redemption¦59658 even after he's sold¦59657 himself¦59657, \add so\add* one of his¦59663 brothers¦59663 can \add ≈buy him¦59664 back\add*,
\v 49 or his uncle or \add ≈cousin\add* or another relative¦59676 from the clan¦59678 can \add ≈pay to free him\add*. \add ≈In fact,\add* he might even be able to \add ≈buy his own way out\add*.
